# multi-constraint-placement-optimisation

Advising on DryWave 3000 unit placement requires simultaneously evaluating multiple independent physical constraints: airflow distribution (no short-circuiting, unobstructed pathways), condensate drainage (outlet lower than unit base), acoustic isolation (away from resonant surfaces such as bed frames and headboards), humidity distribution coverage (reposition if localised pockets persist), and intake surface protection (no steam condensation risk). Satisfying one constraint does not imply the others are met; all must be checked together before finalising any placement recommendation.

## Procedures
- Enumerate all five constraint categories before recommending a position: airflow path, drainage gradient, acoustic environment, humidity distribution, and intake surface conditions.
- Confirm the condensate outlet will be positioned lower than the unit base level.
- Confirm the chosen location is free from acoustically resonant surfaces.
- After placement, monitor for localised humidity pockets and advise repositioning if any persist.
- Warn against positions where steam condensation could reach the air intake surface.

